okay today I'm going to talk to you  about displacement reactions a  displacement reaction is when a more  reactive element takes the place of a  less reactive element we're gonna have a  look at example right here so let's  imagine that this is a solution in a  test tube or a boiling tube of copper  sulfate and what we have here is we have  an iron nail so I've put in the the I  male into copper sulfate to see if  there's going to be some kind of  reaction so let's just write out this  word equation to begin with so I've got  copper sulfate and I'm added that to  iron now in order to see if a reaction  is going to take place I need to have a  look at the reactivity series so if you  remember the reactivity series just has  the most reactive elements near the top  and the least reactive elements near the  bottom so the two things I'm going to be  looking at is comparing copper to iron  so there is iron and there is copper so  because iron is closer to the top it is  more reactive than copper so what is  going to happen is that iron is going to  displace the copper means it takes its  place so like this now what we're left  with is going to be this because the  iron has displaced got rid of the copper  kicked it out we now have iron sulfate  so that is one that's the name of the  sorts that is produced iron sulfate but  we still a copper is now all by itself  so it's iron sulfate plus copper this is  a displacement reaction the more  reactive element has replaced a less  reactive one just going to quickly do  the word equation underneath just so we  have it in both forms and plus Fe goes  to iron sulfate  plus copper okay  now let's have a look at a different  example let's imagine now we had zinc  sulfate instead of copper sulfate and  this time let's imagine we reacting it  with copper so let's have a little look  at the reactivity series to see if  there's going to be a reaction so  remember the most reactive metal so just  move that cross the most reactive metals  are near at the top so if we're  comparing zinc with copper we can see  that zinc is more reactive of copper  because it's further that way  as a result copper cannot displace the  zinc it's not reactive enough it's less  reactive than zinc so if I added copper  to zinc sulfate there would be  absolutely no reaction it's not reactive  enough similarly if I had copper sulfate  let's imagine I've got copper sulfate  like in the first example but I added  copper to it because they have the same  reactivity obviously it's the same  element because it's the same reactivity  again you would also see no reaction as  well so in order for a displacement  reaction to take place we must have a  more reactive element most of of more  reactive elements such as iron reacting  with a less reactive element like copper  so these two would be a good example and  that is just how displacement reactions  work they're an example of a redox  reaction and if you'd like to learn more  about redox reactions please watch the  video about redox reactions thank you  very much
 

